{
  "purpose": "Observe automatic source-format transitions under real constrained-network conditions without preset commands",
  "passed": false,
  "repetitionsPerCase": 1,
  "productionPolicyChanged": false,
  "scope": "H.264, one encoder, authenticated source control and WHEP, identical producer/distributor/browser images, 20-second warmup, 45-second baseline, 45-second capacity limit and 90-second recovery. The original freeze/drop/bitrate gates remain; decoded cadence follows confirmed configured formats. No latency pixel probe is enabled.",
  "interpretation": [
    "Adaptive MediaMTX at 4 Mbit/s passes the full cycle: 720p30 to 540p24 and back to 720p30, one encoder/session, no observed media-time regression, 1.416 s capacity-transition freezes and no subsequent freezes. One repetition does not establish a general performance guarantee.",
    "At 1.5 Mbit/s, both direct and adaptive MediaMTX correctly switch to 360p15 but remain there after 90 seconds of recovery. The direct case additionally fails the unchanged 3-second transition-freeze limit (3.231 s); the MediaMTX case has 2.316 s transition freezes.",
    "Changing only the test content from SMPTE to snow does not resolve recovery; the stream also remains at 360p15. This does not establish a content-limited cause.",
    "Removing only the 10% encoder increase threshold restores the bitrate to 6 Mbit/s and reaches 540p24, but does not finish the return to 720p within the observation window. All other gates pass. The production threshold is unchanged; the experiment is not accepted as a complete fix.",
    "The automatic format worker and real source/decoder switching are exercised. Remaining work is the slower bitrate/format recovery, with retained gates and bounded queues; no causal claim about application-limited estimation or a specific GCC correction is established.",
    "Resource statistics cover each complete scenario. They do not isolate the CPU benefit of a format or establish capture-to-display latency."
  ],
